\title{Constant intensity of a dot product}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1786316438837}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{Why is a dot product #{x \cdot y} essentially always comms-bound?}{
  For #{\mathrm{bf16}[N] \times \mathrm{bf16}[N] \to \mathrm{bf16}[1]} the work is #{N} multiplies and #{N - 1} adds while the traffic is #{2N + 2N + 2} bytes, so

  ##{\mathrm{Intensity} = \frac{2N - 1}{4N + 2} \longrightarrow \frac{1}{2}}

  The point is that the intensity is \em{constant in #{N}}: there is no size you can grow into to escape it, unlike a matmul whose intensity rises with the batch. Half a FLOP per byte sits far below any ridge point — even the TPU v5p VPU, at roughly #{7 \times 10^{12}} FLOPs/s per core, wants an intensity near #{3}.

  Reuse is what buys intensity. Every byte loaded into a matmul feeds many FLOPs; in a dot product it feeds one.
}
